text:
What came out
Suggestions revealed the most, and observers only half caught it. When the type of each utterance was added to the model, suggestions (advice, recommendations, proposals) were the utterances closest to what speakers reported thinking (on average closer than questions), with strong evidence for both speakers. For observers’ guesses the point estimate lands in the same place but the evidence does not hold up: the interval reaches zero, and suggestions were rare enough in the recordings that the estimate rests on a few hundred utterances.

da = FileAttachment("data/dialogue_act_pooled.csv").csv({typed: true})
actOrder = ["suggestion", "question", "inform", "functional", "disagreement", "agreement", "acknowledgment"]
actLabel = new Map([["suggestion", "Suggestion"], ["question", "Question"], ["inform", "Statement"], ["functional", "Functional"], ["disagreement", "Disagreement"], ["agreement", "Agreement"], ["acknowledgment", "Acknowledgment"]])
sideLabel = new Map([["perceiver", "vs what observers guessed"], ["target", "vs what speakers were thinking"]])
// pool the two speakers (inverse-variance), then express each type as its distance from the average of the other six
daPooled = d3.groups(da, d =&gt; d.side, d =&gt; d.dialogue_act).flatMap(([side, acts]) =&gt; {
  const rows = acts.map(([act, g]) =&gt; {
    const w = g.map(d =&gt; 1 / Math.pow((d.upper - d.lower) / 3.92, 2));
    const m = d3.sum(g.map((d, i) =&gt; d.mean * w[i])) / d3.sum(w);
    return {side, act, m, se: Math.sqrt(1 / d3.sum(w))};
  });
  return rows.map(r =&gt; {
    const others = rows.filter(o =&gt; o.act !== r.act);
    const base = d3.mean(others, o =&gt; o.m);
    return {side: sideLabel.get(side), act: actLabel.get(r.act), dev: r.m - base, lo: r.m - base - 1.96 * r.se, hi: r.m - base + 1.96 * r.se, m: r.m, base};
  });
})
Plot.plot({
  width: 720, height: 300, marginLeft: 105, marginRight: 20,
  y: {label: null, domain: actOrder.map(a =&gt; actLabel.get(a))},
  x: {label: "Similarity, relative to the average of the other six utterance types →", domain: [-0.06, 0.08], grid: true, tickFormat: d =&gt; (d &gt; 0 ? "+" : "") + d.toFixed(2)},
  color: {domain: ["vs what observers guessed", "vs what speakers were thinking"], range: ["#8a8f98", "#123F36"], legend: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "#374151"}),
    ...["vs what observers guessed", "vs what speakers were thinking"].map((sd, k) =&gt; Plot.ruleY(daPooled.filter(d =&gt; d.side === sd), {y: "act", x1: "lo", x2: "hi", stroke: "side", strokeWidth: 2, dy: k === 0 ? -5 : 5})),
    ...["vs what observers guessed", "vs what speakers were thinking"].map((sd, k) =&gt; Plot.dot(daPooled.filter(d =&gt; d.side === sd), {y: "act", x: "dev", fill: "side", r: 4.5, dy: k === 0 ? -5 : 5, tip: true, title: d =&gt; `${d.act}, ${d.side}\n${d.dev &gt;= 0 ? "+" : ""}${d.dev.toFixed(3)} relative to the other types (95% interval ${d.lo.toFixed(3)} to ${d.hi.toFixed(3)})\npooled mean ${d.m.toFixed(3)}, other types average ${d.base.toFixed(3)}`}))
  ]
})












































How much each utterance type stands out from the rest, on each side of the comparison. Zero is the average of the other six types; a point to the right means that type matched more closely than the others did. Both speakers are pooled, since who spoke made no difference. Suggestions are the only type whose interval clears zero on the speakers’ side (green). On the observers’ side (grey) the point is in the same place but the interval reaches back to zero, and it rests on only 801 suggestion utterances across four studies, so the evidence there is thin. Intervals reflect each type’s own uncertainty.

The most recent words carry the signal. Similarity rises toward the end of the segment whether the comparison is with the observer’s guess or with the speaker’s own report. That second part is what makes the first part useful. If observers simply echoed the last thing said, the trend would show up on their side only. It shows up on both, so the end of a segment holds the information and observers are reading it.
Who said it does not matter. The speaker’s own words were no closer to the guess, or to the thought, than the partner’s words. This held in every live-conversation study and at every point in the segment. A null on both sides is worth having. It means a model of what someone is thinking should read the whole exchange, the partner’s turns included.

What I found
Partisanship is legible in nonpolitical self-description. People who share bio vocabulary about jobs, hobbies, faith, and family generally also share partisan lean closely enough that a usable audience segmentation comes out of profile text alone (with no third-party data). However, because the liberal bio vocabulary is more diffuse, a strict cutoff may reinforce asymmetry in the dataset. For example, in one of the subsamples, using the strictest cutoff keeps 63,500 conservative-sounding users but only 28,600 liberal-sounding ones.
The two sides bring different moral lenses to the same event. The heatmap gives the ratio of conservative-leaning to liberal-leaning authors’ per-token rate for each moral foundation in each sample. Note: this is on a log scale so a doubling and a halving are the same distance from center.

ml = FileAttachment("data/moral_by_lean.csv").csv({typed: true})
foundations = ["care", "harm", "fairness", "cheating", "loyalty", "betrayal", "authority", "subversion", "sanctity", "degradation", "outrage_proxy"]
foundLabel = new Map([["care", "Care"], ["harm", "Harm"], ["fairness", "Fairness"], ["cheating", "Cheating"], ["loyalty", "Loyalty"], ["betrayal", "Betrayal"], ["authority", "Authority"], ["subversion", "Subversion"], ["sanctity", "Sanctity"], ["degradation", "Degradation"], ["outrage_proxy", "Outrage proxy"]])
studies = ["Cancel Culture", "Is Over Party", "Academics", "Celebrities", "Civilians"]
ratio = studies.flatMap(s =&gt; {
  const con = ml.find(d =&gt; d.study === s && d.lean === "Conservative");
  const lib = ml.find(d =&gt; d.study === s && d.lean === "Liberal");
  return foundations.map(f =&gt; ({study: s, foundation: foundLabel.get(f), con: con[f], lib: lib[f], log2ratio: Math.log2(con[f] / lib[f])}));
})
Plot.plot({
  width: 720, height: 300, marginLeft: 110, marginBottom: 60,
  x: {label: null, domain: foundations.map(f =&gt; foundLabel.get(f)), tickRotate: -30},
  y: {label: null, domain: studies},
  color: {type: "diverging", scheme: "RdBu", reverse: true, domain: [-1, 1], legend: true, width: 360, label: "ratio, conservative / liberal (← liberal higher · conservative higher →)", ticks: [-0.7, 0, 0.7], tickFormat: d =&gt; d === 0 ? "equal" : d &gt; 0 ? `${Math.pow(2, d).toFixed(1)}× con` : `${Math.pow(2, -d).toFixed(1)}× lib`},
  marks: [
    Plot.cell(ratio, {x: "foundation", y: "study", fill: "log2ratio", inset: 1, tip: true, title: d =&gt; `${d.study}, ${d.foundation}\nconservative: ${(d.con * 100).toFixed(2)}% of tokens\nliberal: ${(d.lib * 100).toFixed(2)}% of tokens\nratio: ${Math.pow(2, d.log2ratio).toFixed(2)}`}),
    Plot.text(ratio, {x: "foundation", y: "study", text: d =&gt; Math.pow(2, d.log2ratio).toFixed(2), fill: d =&gt; Math.abs(d.log2ratio) &gt; 0.6 ? "white" : "#111", fontSize: 11})
  ]
})












































Ratio of conservative-leaning to liberal-leaning authors’ per-token rate of each moral foundation, by sample. Red means conservatives use the foundation more; blue means liberals do. Hover for the underlying rates.

Fairness showed the largest partisan gap of any foundation, but its direction depended on who was being canceled. Conservative-leaning authors used fairness language about twice as often as liberal-leaning ones when the target was an academic (2.14×) and 1.7× as often for civilians, and slightly less often in the other three samples. Cheating language leaned liberal in four of five samples. Betrayal, degradation, and the outrage proxy leaned conservative in nearly every sample, the outrage proxy by 1.07× to 1.17×. Some of this matches what Moral Foundations Theory predicts and some does not (sanctity, expected to run conservative, leaned liberal in four of five samples), which is a reason to read the heatmap one sample at a time.
The sides talk about different parts of the story. The topic model recovered themes I could name: free speech and censorship, accountability and consequences, racism and lost jobs, boycotts and party politics, and the history of the term itself, often cast as “the mob.” Conservative-leaning authors spent more of their tweets on the history-and-mob topic (13% vs. 10%) and on free speech (17% vs. 15%); liberal-leaning authors spent more on accountability and consequences (8% vs. 6%). The gaps are modest (the largest is d = 0.16). It is the same event, discussed in different vocabularies.
Anger and moral outrage are different measurements. Tweets by conservative-leaning authors scored slightly higher on the transformer’s anger (d = 0.14); tweets by liberal-leaning authors scored slightly higher on negative sentiment (d = 0.14) and irony (d = 0.11). The more useful result is a null: tweet by tweet, the anger score and the moral-outrage measure are essentially uncorrelated (r = −.05 across 8.6 million tweets). A team that picked one of them off the shelf would get a different answer than a team that picked the other.
Outrage arrives after the attention.

ev = FileAttachment("data/outrage_event_time.csv").csv({typed: true})
evTypes = ["Academics", "Celebrities", "Civilians"]
evBase = new Map(evTypes.map(t =&gt; {
  const pre = ev.filter(d =&gt; d.type === t && d.rel &lt; 0);
  return [t, d3.sum(pre, d =&gt; d.outrage * d.n) / d3.sum(pre, d =&gt; d.n)];
}))
evRel = ev.map(d =&gt; ({...d, rel_outrage: d.outrage_7d / evBase.get(d.type)}))
viewof evShow = Inputs.checkbox(evTypes, {label: "Show", value: ["Academics", "Celebrities"]})
Plot.plot({
  width: 720, height: 340, marginLeft: 55, marginRight: 20,
  x: {label: "Days from the day of peak tweet volume →", domain: [-30, 30], ticks: [-30, -20, -10, 0, 10, 20, 30]},
  y: {label: "↑ Outrage rate relative to the month before", grid: true, domain: [0, 2.5], tickFormat: d =&gt; d + "×"},
  color: {domain: evTypes, range: ["#123F36", "#C49A45", "#8a8f98"], legend: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([1], {stroke: "#374151", strokeDasharray: "3,3"}),
    Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: "#374151"}),
    Plot.line(evRel.filter(d =&gt; evShow.includes(d.type)), {x: "rel", y: "rel_outrage", stroke: "type", strokeWidth: 2.5, curve: "monotone-x", tip: true,
      title: d =&gt; `${d.type}, day ${d.rel &gt; 0 ? "+" : ""}${d.rel}\n7-day outrage rate ${d.outrage_7d.toFixed(1)} per 1,000 words (${d.rel_outrage.toFixed(2)}× the month before)\n${d.n.toLocaleString()} tweets that day`})
  ]
})












































Each target’s tweets were aligned to that target’s own peak-volume day (day 0), then pooled by type of target, weighted by volume, and smoothed over seven days. The y-axis is the rate of outrage-proxy words relative to the same group’s average in the 30 days before. Civilians are hidden by default; with six targets and few tweets per day away from the peak, their curve is mostly noise.

The burst of attention comes first; the condemnation builds behind it. For academics, outrage crested about ten days after the peak-volume day at roughly twice its pre-event rate. For celebrities it rose with the burst, peaked within the first week, and dropped back within about ten days. Averaged across targets, outrage ran higher in the month after the peak than in the month before for all three kinds of case (57 targets).
Outrage did not buy reach across the board. In the 2023 SPSP analysis (a 1.05-million-tweet subset scored with LIWC-22), anger and impoliteness predicted retweets most strongly for mid-tier “macro-influencer” accounts, more than for celebrities. In the full corpus, the outrage measure and retweet counts are essentially unrelated overall (r = .01 across 1.5 million tweets). Whatever engagement outrage earns appears to depend on who is posting it.
